/**
@page driver_block Block devices
@brief Sector storage for filesystems, and the ramdisk driver.

@tableofcontents

A block device stores 512-byte sectors and has no `/dev` file: a board's
`mounts` section attaches a filesystem to it, and the generator builds the
::block_device_t that the filesystem receives. The interface is documented in
@ref block; how filesystems use it is in @ref storage.

| Compatible | Storage | Boards |
| --- | --- | --- |
| @ref binding_homecore_ramdisk "homecore,ramdisk" | A `.bss` buffer; lost at reset | LM3S6965EVB (32 KB, littlefs), STM32F4DISCOVERY (64 KB FAT, 16 KB littlefs) |

@section driver_block_contract Operations

| Operation | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| `read(device, sector, buf, count)` | Copy `count` whole sectors into `buf`; 0, or -1 with `errno` |
| `write(device, sector, buf, count)` | Store `count` whole sectors; 0, or -1 with `errno` |
| `sync(device)` | Finish pending writes |
| `sector_count(device)` | The device size in sectors |

A range past the end of the device fails with `EIO` and transfers nothing;
a `NULL` buffer fails with `EFAULT`. Operations run in thread context only.
There is no erase: current devices overwrite in place, and littlefs's erase
callback does nothing.

@section driver_block_ramdisk Ramdisk

@code{.yaml}
devices:
  ram0: {compatible: "homecore,ramdisk", size: 32K}
mounts:
  /ram: {device: ram0, fs: littlefs, format: true}
@endcode

The `size` property, a multiple of 512, sizes a 4-byte-aligned buffer that the
generator places in `.bss`, so it counts as static RAM in the linker map and
reduces the heap by the same amount. The buffer starts zeroed, which holds no
filesystem, so a ramdisk mount uses `format: true`. Transfers are `memcpy`
calls; the ramdisk has no interrupts or hardware.

@section driver_block_tests Tests and validation

`tests/fat_test.c` and `tests/littlefs_test.c` run the filesystems on
ramdisks, up to a full volume, and `tests/littlefs_test.c` wraps the ramdisk in
a device that fails writes to simulate resets. The ramdisk's own range and
buffer checks have no dedicated test. The QEMU regression uses the LM3S6965EVB ramdisk through
littlefs.
*/
